  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers are expected tonight into Monday, with best \nchances for showers across Central NY as a slow moving Nor'easter \ngrazes the area.\n\nCurrent satellite imagery displays a mature anomalously strong low \npressure system, centered over the southern Jersey shore, with bands \nof rain showers now pushing into CNY and the Finger Lakes region. \nThis low will continue to fill as it wobbles over the Atlantic \nwaters and inland to southern New England through Tuesday. \n\nAs an inverted surface trough associated with this surface low \ntracks slowly westward, we should see rain showers continue their \nwestward expansion through the evening, and to perhaps the far \nwestern NYS border. Rainfall is expected to be minor, with perhaps a \nthird of an inch of rain in the Finger Lakes region...and less \nelsewhere. Rain chances will quickly taper down by late evening \nhours tonight, with a few spotty light rain showers remaining across \nthe North Country through Monday afternoon. \n\nAs surface high begins to press eastward tonight and Monday as the \nsurface low spirals eastward, rain showers will diminish, though the \nnorthern flow and lingering low level moisture will likely maintain \na blanket of clouds over our region through at least Monday night, \nwith perhaps some sunshine emerging Tuesday.   \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warming trend mid-week ahead of the next rain shower \nbearing cold front.\n\nOur wind flow will become southerly by Wednesday of this week, \nallowing a warm autumn airmass to push into our region. In \nconjunction with a ridge aloft, and 850 hPa temperatures rising into \nthe upper teens, surface high temperatures will become some 10 \ndegrees or more above normal, with Thursday the likely warmest day \nthis week. Models have slowed a bit with the approach of the next \ncold front with the suite of ensembles (ENS, GEFS, and GEPS) now \nfocusing highest chances for measurable rainfall now Thursday night. \nThis rainfall is also expected to be light, with chances for over a \ntenth of an inch of rain in a 6-hour window generally less than 25 \npercent.",
